.TH RECIPE-RECIPE 5 "2026-11-08" "Recipe" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
recipe-recipe \- managed API 2 Rhai recipe contract
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every executable recipe declares const RECIPE_API = 2, its own literal const
NEEDS map, and initializes ctx with
current package facts. Setup executes once per invocation inside Ginux.
Pure imported facts modules need no executable lifecycle.
.SH RUNTIME AND STATE
Setup reads immutable RUNTIME. Hooks receive the same ctx.runtime together with
ctx.state and ctx.outputs. Runtime paths are:
.TP
.B paths.inputs
/inputs, explicitly imported immutable files/trees.
.TP
.B paths.build
/work/build, owned acquisition/build work.
.TP
.B paths.tools
/work/tools, private copies of declared dependency tools.
.TP
.B paths.dependencies
Immutable direct dependency products at /inputs/recipe-dependencies/DEP/OUTPUT.
.TP
.B paths.temp
/work/tmp, owned scratch.
.TP
.B paths.output, paths.target
/out, owned product/installed-target tree.
.TP
.B paths.recipe_dir, paths.base_recipe_dir
Staged current/base recipe directories.
.TP
.B paths.prefix
The folder the package output will live in once activated:
PROFILE/.packages/NAME, through the profile symlink of the selected store.
Unit with --no-activate, for a dependency, or when the recipe's name is not
a literal. It is part of the saved-state identity, so a build belongs to the
store it was made for, and activation refuses a package built for another
folder.
.PP
runtime.target.os and .arch describe the selected execution target.
runtime.host.libc is the C library of the system the package will be installed on:
"glibc", "musl" or "unknown". Recipe decides it from the system's dynamic loaders,
not the worker's image; both or neither present is "unknown". A recipe declares
the C libraries its build works with in ABOUT.libc, a list from "glibc" and "musl"
(absent means any); ABOUT.libc_carried true says its package brings its own C
library and loader. Before an install, Recipe compares the two and, when they
differ, states the facts and the flag that installs anyway, \fB\-\-any\-libc\fR.
The comparison is advice with that override.
runtime.options includes jobs, force_acquire, force_build, force_install and
persist_state. Package versions, URLs and digests remain ordinary recipe facts.
.PP
Only ctx.state is serialized/restored, with matching filesystem checkpoints.
Current setup, runtime and named outputs are not overwritten by an old context.
State values must be JSON-compatible, with finite numbers; unsupported values
fail with their field path. Readiness still checks real files/products.
.SH CHECKS AND PHASES
Checks is_installed(ctx), is_built(ctx) and is_acquired(ctx) return ready(ctx) or
needs_work(ctx, nonempty_reason). Exceptions and malformed results fail execution.
Ordinary context maps and booleans are not readiness results.
.PP
Install checks installation first, then determines build/acquisition work. It
executes required dependencies and phases, rechecking custom readiness after
success. acquire(ctx), build(ctx), install(ctx) and remove(ctx) return updated
context maps. Prebuilt packages can omit build work. Missing required work fails
instead of silently succeeding.
.PP
Optional cleanup(ctx, reason) returns context. Reasons are manual and
auto.phase.success or auto.phase.failure. Guard cleanup of useful products/cache
by reason. Errors after success fail the command; errors while handling failure
are reported with the original failure. Successful failure cleanup is checkpointed
with its returned state and matching files; the producer still fails without
publication. No no-op cleanup stub is required.
.SH INPUTS AND OUTPUTS
NEEDS.inputs maps names to file, directory, file? or directory?; ? permits omission.
Callers bind exactly declared inputs with --input NAME=PATH read-only at
/inputs/NAME; DEP::NAME binds a dependency input. Defines select genuine
recipe facts/choices; they do not grant host filesystem access. A named target
input seeds /out for fresh state; retained state restores that owned target.
Compiler-image files cannot establish installation in the selected product.
.PP
NEEDS.outputs maps exact product names to file or directory. Select those actual
files/directories under assigned /out or /work using ctx.outputs. Completed
installation and installed reuse must supply the complete declaration.
Intermediate phases may expose a subset; every present output is checked before
checkpointing/publication.
Archive modification times have whole-second precision. Target ownership,
special modes and capabilities/xattrs travel through the validated target
metadata manifest, independently of staging privileges; see docs/target-metadata.md.
Consumers validate required names and kinds. Successful execution plus validated,
bounded archive publication returns absolute host output paths in final JSON.
Hooks continue to use domain paths. Imported inputs and earlier publications
remain immutable across remove, cleanup and changed-input invocations.
.SH ACQUISITION AND COMMANDS
Downloads use download(url, exact_dest, sha256[, resume_bool]); the full SHA256 is
required and staged bytes are checked before replacing a valid destination.
Pinned Git uses git_checkout(url, exact_dest, full_commit), requiring a full
40/64-hex commit. Endpoint grants and limits belong in literal NEEDS; caller
flags can only narrow them. Explicit restrictions cap every dependency's own
limits and intersect its endpoints; no grant is lent. NEEDS.network_max_bytes
defaults to 8 GiB.
.PP
exec_checked and exec_output fail on nonzero; exec returns a status. Their
argument arrays avoid shell parsing. Optional cwd/env maps affect only that
child. Shell pipelines must check every producer/extractor failure. Commands
and helpers execute inside Ginux and require suitable image/tools support.
.SH DEPENDENCIES AND INHERITANCE
Literal deps and build_deps declarations select recipes from the search path.
They use the same lifecycle in separate Ginux instances, retaining separate
state/files pairs and their own NEEDS. No parent grant is lent. Declared products
are immutable consumer inputs; a tools directory output contributes to its
private /work/tools prefix.
.PP
The leading declaration //! extends: base.rhai compiles/merges the base first;
child functions of the same name/arity override it. Setup runs base-first.
Literal module paths are preserved. Cycles and dynamic import/dependency
declarations fail preparation. Discovery never evaluates setup on the host.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
const RECIPE_API = 2;
const NEEDS = #{ outputs: #{ program: "file" } };
let ctx = #{name: "hello", program: "/out/hello"};
fn is_acquired(ctx) { ready(ctx) }
fn acquire(ctx) { ctx }
fn is_built(ctx) { ready(ctx) }
fn is_installed(ctx) {
    if !is_file(ctx.program) { return needs_work(ctx, "missing hello"); }
    ctx.outputs.program = ctx.program;
    ready(ctx)
}
fn install(ctx) {
    write_file(ctx.program, "hello\n");
    ctx.outputs.program = ctx.program;
    ctx
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
recipe(1), recipe-helpers(7), WRITING_RECIPES.md
